{"skill":{"slug":"web-pilot","displayName":"Web Pilot"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1771349856982,"changelog":"Initial release: browser automation & accessibility skill with 20+ actions — search, browse, interact, screenshot, form fill, JS eval, PDF export","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":8774,"sha256":"b91950a4cb686dc14415a5eddbbbc00e0a9700164be7af5e3ebef612d445114e","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":2797,"sha256":"c6930beb2bd8a68b65b1f3ccc14fc4176d9835da6b0700a44d9c03cd1ff75801","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/browser_session.py","size":32768,"sha256":"ebab8b32c4c10d576d656bb533995ba3af9e8373f1e30f8daeecf6ff3307824e","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/download_file.py","size":5969,"sha256":"d8c7778777f109de64317d9cae45f58aadbc4ff7ee0b9868c4e059f06db0c311","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/google_search.py","size":6468,"sha256":"2a82d8ccb03d64509355919ddae8b2e547c7504d62ae0a8acf556b7730d9c5bc","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/read_page.py","size":6556,"sha256":"5b4392e1e7f9046049c23db96f26cc9547e0b7ea3ad38b349480f2597be8db6d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2444,"sha256":"8cd91c1ea7611e6ceb18f76807dde63633802120cc33196fb704236acb785eeb","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789071449561,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"a49f44cf97f66885e4f2d93e20538577b6916ec43e64ef69ec64de18180f0be6","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/a49f44cf97f66885e4f2d93e20538577b6916ec43e64ef69ec64de18180f0be6","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: web-pilot\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle is classified as suspicious due to several high-risk capabilities that, while part of its stated functionality, introduce significant vulnerabilities if misused. Specifically, `scripts/download_file.py` and `scripts/browser_session.py` allow writing files (downloads, screenshots, PDFs) to arbitrary paths on the host system, which could lead to arbitrary file write vulnerabilities. Additionally, `scripts/browser_session.py` includes an `eval` action that permits arbitrary JavaScript execution within the browser context, posing a risk for client-side data theft or browser exploitation. `scripts/download_file.py` also weakens security by falling back to `verify=False` for SSL errors. These are powerful primitives that could be exploited via prompt injection against the AI agent, but there is no clear evidence of intentional malicious behavior (e.g., covert exfiltration, persistence mechanisms) within the code itself.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779164891030},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":25,"checkedAt":1789071449561},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This web automation skill largely matches its stated purpose, but it needs Review because it gives broad browser and download authority with unsafe defaults and weak local controls.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Arbitrary outbound web requests are expected for this skill, but unrestricted URL fetching/navigation, proxy support, automatic TLS-verification fallback, and unsanitized download filenames exceed a well-contained browsing/download workflow.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Installation uses ordinary pip and Playwright Chromium installation commands, but dependencies and browser artifacts are not pinned or hash-locked, so installs are not reproducible.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The artifact exposes arbitrary URL navigation, clicks, form submission, JavaScript evaluation, downloads, and cookie-consent clicks without a clear confirmation boundary, domain scope, or read-only mode for sensitive/authenticated sites.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The persistent browser is disclosed, but it runs as a long-lived child process controlled through a predictable /tmp Unix socket without explicit authentication or private socket permissions, enabling local session-control risk.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The search, page reading, persistent browser, clicking, screenshots, downloads, and cookie handling fit the web-pilot purpose; however the main SKILL.md under-describes implemented high-impact actions such as form fill/submit, arbitrary JavaScript evaluation, PDF export, proxy use, and broader filesystem writes.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review this skill carefully before installing. It is a powerful browser automation tool, not just a reader: use it only in isolated environments or with trusted tasks, avoid authenticated or sensitive websites unless you are supervising actions, do not submit forms without explicit review, avoid secrets in search queries, and treat downloaded files as untrusted. The download and persistent-browser implementation should be hardened before use in shared or high-trust environments.","findings":"[TP4] expected: The broad browser automation capability is coherent with the README and purpose, but the primary SKILL.md omits several sensitive actions, so the mismatch concern is partially supported rather than evidence of deception.\n[SDI-1] expected: Form filling/submission and JavaScript evaluation fit an automation browser, but they are high-impact on arbitrary sites and lack clear confirmation or scoping controls.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: Retrying downloads with certificate verification disabled is not required for the stated purpose and materially weakens download integrity.\n[SQP-2] expected: Cookie dismissal and form interaction are disclosed, but automatic broad consent-clicking and optional submission can change third-party site state without an explicit user decision at action time.\n[LP3] unexpected: The skill needs network and filesystem access, but the artifact has only minimal skill metadata and no explicit permission scoping for those side effects.\n[E1] expected: Sending search queries to DuckDuckGo, Brave, or Google is expected for a web-search skill and is disclosed, though users should avoid placing secrets in queries.\n[MP2] expected: The cited 'fill input' text describes a form-fill feature, not context-window stuffing; this finding is not supported by the artifact.\n[SQP-3] unexpected: Hard-coded en-US/English settings are low-impact product limitations, not a major security concern.\n[TM3] unexpected: The verify=False retry is an unsafe default and supports Review, especially because the tool writes downloaded content to disk.\n[T09] unexpected: The static scan's arbitrary file-write concern is supported: download filenames from user input or response headers are joined to the output directory without sanitization or containment checks.\n[T05] expected: Arbitrary URL access is expected for browsing, but there is no scheme, host, private-network, or redirect validation, which is risky when untrusted content can influence tool arguments.\n[T07] unexpected: The unauthenticated fixed /tmp browser-session socket is not required for the user-facing purpose and creates avoidable local session hijack risk.\n[T08] expected: The dependencies are mainstream and purpose-aligned, but unpinned installation remains a supply-chain hardening gap rather than evidence of malicious behavior.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789071449561}}}}}